# Flutter UI Rules

## 1. Presentation Responsibility

Presentation code is responsible for:

- rendering
- navigation
- user interaction
- visual validation
- displaying application state

It must not own backend business rules.

## 2. Widget Size

Avoid very large widgets.

When a widget becomes difficult to understand or test, extract focused components.

Prefer:

Page
→ Section
→ Component
→ Primitive

over monolithic widgets.

## 3. Reusability

Extract shared UI components only when reuse or semantic clarity justifies it.

Do not create abstractions prematurely.

## 4. Theme

Use the centralized application theme.

Do not hardcode colors, typography, or spacing repeatedly.

## 5. Responsive Layout

Layouts must account for supported device sizes.

Do not assume one screen resolution.

## 6. Accessibility

Interactive controls must have meaningful:

- labels
- semantic descriptions where needed
- adequate touch targets
- readable text

## 7. Loading

Every asynchronous screen must define its loading behavior.

Avoid blank screens during requests.

## 8. Empty States

Empty collections must have an intentional UI state.

Do not treat empty data as an error.

## 9. Errors

Errors must be understandable to the user.

Do not display raw exceptions, stack traces, or backend implementation details.

## 10. Navigation

Navigation must use the centralized routing strategy.

Do not introduce ad-hoc navigation architectures inside features.

## 11. BuildContext

Do not retain `BuildContext` beyond its safe lifecycle.

Avoid passing BuildContext deep into domain/application code.

## 12. Internationalization

User-facing text must not be scattered as unexplained literals when the application supports localization.

Keep localization strategy centralized.
